The six files sit side by side here, flat. Each is its source byte for
byte except its import lines, and the rewrite is four rules and no more:
  - the kernel's paths (../../kernel/{calendar,money}/<file>.ts) become
    @hm/kernel/{calendar,money} (the same bytes; packages/kernel/VENDORED_FROM);
  - a sibling's ./<file>.ts becomes ./<file>.js;
  - ../boarding/<file>.ts becomes ./<file>.js, because the boarding files
    sit beside the PMI ones here;
  - ../transfers/ops-1-7.ts becomes ../lossmit-stub.js.
Nothing else may be edited: src/__tests__/pmi.test.ts rewrites each
original's imports by those rules, compares the result and the rest of the
file, and fails on any difference. Lint and Prettier skip this directory for
the reason they skip the vendored trees.

One file the port needs is ours: ../lossmit-stub.ts, outside this
directory. types.ts imports the inherited loss-mitigation file's type from
transfers/ops-1-7.ts, which is stateful and does not port; nothing graded
reads that field, so the stub stands in for its type.

Not ported: pmi/index.ts and pmi/ops.ts (ops.ts reads zoned Eastern
wall-clock time for the LAR 89 deadlines and investor/period.ts's Fannie Mae
business days), and pmi/denial.ts (the denial notice's content and renewal
date, which v1 does not grade).

The conventions the environment also follows (the trigger, the midpoint's
basis, the remaining term at an ARM reset, the premium-stop anchor, the
refund's coverage period, the date the threshold was reached) live in the
servicing app's stateful service (app/tools/section10.ts) or nowhere; they
are restated in src/pmi/grader.ts with the lines they come from.

To take a change from the servicing app, copy the six files again and apply
the same four rewrites.
